          }
        },
        {
          "extraction_class": "individual_presentation",
          "extraction_text": "spasms of increased muscle stiffness and rigidity lasting up to several minutes, precipitated by sudden movement, noises, touch, or emotional upset; spasms may cause profuse sweating and a rise in blood pressure; persistent rigidity of the back, abdomen, and limbs between spasms; distal musculature and facial musculature usually spared; symptoms may progress over months to years",
          "char_interval": {
            "start_pos": 8970,
            "end_pos": 9010
          },
          "alignment_status": "AlignmentStatus.MATCH_LESSER",
          "extraction_index": 1,
          "group_index": 0,
          "description": null,
          "attributes": {},
          "_token_interval": {
            "start_index": 1775,
            "end_index": 1781
          }
        },
        {
          "extraction_class": "individual_presentation",
          "extraction_text": "fixed lumbar hyperlordosis and a “permanent ‘shrug’ of the shoulders with the neck drawn down into the shoulder girdle”; hypertrophy of the paraspinal musculature in long-standing cases; affected muscles reveal a tight, board-like quality, most often in the paravertebral and abdominal areas; deep tendon reflexes may be symmetrically increased",
          "char_interval": {
            "start_pos": 10099,
            "end_pos": 10219
          },
          "alignment_status": "AlignmentStatus.MATCH_LESSER",
          "extraction_index": 1,
          "group_index": 0,
          "description": null,
          "attributes": {},
          "_token_interval": {
            "start_index": 2013,
            "end_index": 2035
          }
        },
        {
          "extraction_class": "individual_diagnostics",
          "extraction_text": "Neurophysiologic studies demonstrated continuous discharge of motor-unit potentials resembling a normal voluntary contraction with superimposed generalized paroxysmal muscle contractions comprised of simultaneous contraction of agonistic and antagonistic muscles; motor-unit discharges may be extinguished with IV diazepam, myoneural block, motor nerve block, or general anesthesia; EEG is normal; sleep studies often show long latencies between wakefulness and stage 1 sleep due to muscle spasm; in stage 2, muscle spasms disappear and sleep proceeds normally",
          "char_interval": {
            "start_pos": 10593,
            "end_pos": 10617
          },
          "alignment_status": "AlignmentStatus.MATCH_LESSER",
          "extraction_index": 2,
          "group_index": 1,
          "description": null,
          "attributes": {},
          "_token_interval": {
            "start_index": 2108,
            "end_index": 2110
          }
        },
        {
          "extraction_class": "individual_diagnostics",
          "extraction_text": "Several patients who had stiff-man syndrome have been tested and found to have anti-GAD antibodies, although other patients have not shown this antibody",
